it's time to fight hi this is senior  editor Kevin van ORD standing in for  reviewer Miguel concepción to tell you  about Hyrule warriors  you might be prepared to trash Hyrule  warriors a dynasty Warriors meets Legend  of Zelda mashup no one ever asked for  but once you open that first chest and  Link thrusts that treasure toward the  camera you realize that this is a Legend  of Zelda spin-off worth taking seriously  and you're reminded that sometimes it's  these left-field concepts that make the  most sense the majority of the Warriors  games are basic exercises and tactical  map management that also let you become  the main contributor to the action at  ground level it's there that you slice  your sword through throngs of simple  foes which proves simultaneously  unimaginative and empowering as you  liberate one fortification from enemy  hands  another keep is being invaded the key to  winning often comes down to simply being  faster than your enemy and filling in  the map with your color-coded forces  like the one piece pirate warriors  crossover series Hyrule warriors is more  focused on objectives and less on  removing opposition forces from the map  if you wish you can try to make the game  feel more like a traditional Warriors  game by taking over each fort one at a  time that creates its own challenges  since many of the objectives and Hyrule  warriors have times deadlines but Hyrule  warriors is at its most stimulating when  it tests your management of priorities  as a Zelda fan of course you want to  save the Deku Tree when it's being  invaded on all sides but you're also  close to liberating a keep on one of the  far corners of the map and since you've  just reached a thousand kills a  collectible object has temporarily  appeared on the map make a choice  warrior waffling is for the weak  setting priorities isn't as easy as it  sounds the biggest rush comes when  you've completed all the mid mission  objectives and you're left  racing against the enemy toward the  victory and defeat conditions even if  you confront the boss with a full bar of  Health the same can't be said about the  health of your home base at the other  end of the map should you return to your  base or stay and try to defeat the boss  to end the mission not knowing your HQ s  health unless you're actually inside  your HQ helps add mystery and suspense  to your tactical decisions for all the  Legend of Zelda related items jingles  and familiar faces that are thrown at  you every other second Hyrule warriors  still sticks closely to the typical  dynasty Warriors structure if you have  dynasty Warriors muscle memory then you  know that the first thing to do at the  start of the first mission is to jump  into the fray get to work in liberating  the nearest fort and set yourself on a  path to at least a thousand kills and as  a Warriors game in 2014 Hyrule warriors  reaps the benefits from many of the  lessons and criticisms leveled at prior  games most notably the boss lock-on  option that was introduced a few years  ago  the greatest trick Hyrule warriors pulls  is in convincing Zelda fans that the  game might just belong in the  much-debated Legend of Zelda timeline  further validating this idea is that the  story mode is supported by  interdimensional journeys to various  Legend of Zelda worlds as an argument  for and against the notion of canon  eligibility Hyrule warriors even manages  to rehash key plot points from the  franchise that are also significant  spoilers to the very very few who will  play this game but have never beaten a  Legend of Zelda game every character in  Hyrule warriors modest playable roster  possesses unique attacks and animations  watching link perform spectacular melee  attacks only makes one wish such potent  moves were available in mainline Legend  of Zelda games you may notice however  that Hyrule warriors does not avoid  previous Warriors games archetypes you  can see Omega forces signature  familiarity with wushu martial arts in  some of em posit acts you Connor Lana  with her adorned sleeves exposed midriff  and giant ponytail would not look out of  place in a Dynasty Warriors game  characters aside Hyrule warriors adds  little to the Warriors mega series as a  whole but as one of the more well  produced Warriors game in recent memory  it's easy to see Hyrule warriors opening  the series up to even more spin-offs if  the Warriors franchise has proven  anything it's that almost every  entertainment property is fair game and  that is both exciting and scary  you
 

